# Strength Ledger Engineering Directive

## Ledger Bot: coach-led assistance only

Before Ledger Bot work, read `docs/LEDGER_BOT_POLICY.md`. The owner permanently
prohibits Bot-generated Sessions/programs/prescriptions. Use the closed backend
tool boundary; no model prompt or UI restriction substitutes for enforcement.
Existing-source reuse and explicit coach-directed numeric transformations do not
authorize missing-data synthesis. The initial foundation is DEV-only/read-only.

## Credential boundaries

Before adding credentials or provider client configuration, or handling a secret
alert, read `docs/release/GITHUB_KEY_ALERT_2026-10-13.md` and its permanent
credential handling rule. Private credentials never enter Git or client bundles.
Classify client identifiers and verify actual provider restrictions before push;
keep secret scanning/push protection enabled and never dismiss an alert by filename.

## Complete-request delivery and owner-policy reconciliation

Before implementation, record the complete current request, acceptance criteria,
preserved product laws and required delivery destination in the task's tracked
release/engineering record. Read established owner policy; do not implement one
message fragment while dropping the rest. An explicit owner correction controls.
If product instructions remain ambiguous or conflict without a clear correction,
ask about that specific decision before changing its behavior; continue independent
safe work. Do not invent product decisions or another approval for authorized work.

Follow the **Complete-request Delivery Law** in `docs/RELEASE_INVARIANTS.md`.
Before closeout, account for every requested outcome with exact evidence or an
explicit remaining gap. Separate local test results, publication/push, verified
live source/artifact, and observed device behavior. Never call a local fix shipped,
a pushed commit live, or backend eligibility proof observed phone playback.
No guarantee may exceed the evidence. Respect explicit restrictions on simulators. Follow the owner-directed Mac lock handling in that constitution: continue non-simulator checks and the authorized push if passing; retain honest skipped-check evidence.

Production main pushes require the source-pinned canonical gate installed by
`scripts/install_shared_backend_push_guard.py`. Its independent owner-policy
acceptance suite must reject cross-manufacturer machine PR leakage even if the
candidate rewrites its own tests/gate. Missing/changed trusted checks fail closed;
never remove the hook or refresh pins merely to pass a candidate. Owner product
policy changes need explicit owner evidence before protection changes.


## Efficient delivery and stopping

Follow the **Efficient Delivery and Stopping Rule** in
`docs/RELEASE_INVARIANTS.md` and `docs/release/DELIVERY_EFFICIENCY_2026-10-14.md`.
Scope validation before work; check the governed wrapper before duplicating its
suites. Reuse exact applicable passing evidence for unchanged inputs and shared
runtime behavior; verify only target-specific differences separately. Diagnose
failed checks before retrying, and avoid concurrency that creates contention.
Keep mandatory protections, report verified shipment promptly, finish only
necessary closeout, and stop. Owner stop/resume instructions control immediately;
a temporary stop is not permanent product or runtime retirement.

## Web performance acceptance

Follow the **Web performance release requirement — October 18, 2026** in
`docs/RELEASE_INVARIANTS.md`. Web visual and workflow work also requires measured
loading, responsive controls and asset/cache validation before it is called ready.
Keep the existing backend and real-browser performance regression checks in the
affected DEV/release validation; preserve functionality and access protections.
Distinguish lab evidence, verified live delivery and actual owner observation.

Web workspace visual/workflow passes must cover both Team Coach and Self-Coach.
Reuse shared Athlete Workspace components; validate personal ownership and the
absence of Team-only requests with a fictional Self-Coach browser fixture.
Retain `npm run test:self-coach-web` alongside affected Team Coach checks in
explicit DEV/release validation. A passing Team-only audit does not establish
Self-Coach parity; these checks are not claimed to be automatic push-hook gates.

## Owner Non-Recurrence Directive

Before responding to an owner-designated unacceptable failure, read the
UNACCEPTABLE section of `docs/RELEASE_INVARIANTS.md` and
`docs/OWNER_NON_RECURRENCE_INCIDENTS.md`. Explicit owner `unacceptable` means
`OWNER NON-RECURRENCE DIRECTIVE`: fix the instance AND prevent its technical
failure class from recurring. Determine cause/process gap, audit equivalents,
retain a permanent protection, deliberately failure-test it, and carry it through
DEV/TestFlight/release workflows. Record the incident and all seven required
closeout fields in the repository. Known equivalent failures or unknown counts
prevent closure. Recurrence is also a release/process failure: repair the failed
protection. Product decisions still require explicit owner approval; default NO.

## Mandatory Release Constitution

Read the **Worktree Lifecycle Law** in that same constitution before creating,
using, reconciling, or removing a worktree. Dirty canonical DEV is expected and
must be preserved. Isolation needs a concrete recorded reason; register source
DEV state, starting SHA, purpose, and planned changes. Inventory all worktrees
before cleanup, reconcile valid unique state first, retain ambiguous work for an
owner decision, assess/synchronize legitimate active worktrees, and close every
completed temporary worktree in the same release workflow. Publication without
exact-source DEV back-propagation and verified worktree removal is incomplete.
Use the governed lifecycle tool; never delete unaccounted product/evidence files.

Before any TestFlight/release operation, read and enforce
`docs/RELEASE_INVARIANTS.md`. It is the single authoritative release constitution:
DEV must be the superset; TestFlight is cumulative; explicit owner removals only;
every release-path fix must reach DEV in the same workflow. Gate violations block
publication. Missing valid release state must flow TestFlight → DEV.

Before modifying, auditing, promoting, or validating any Strength Ledger code,
read the governing architecture document:

`docs/Strength_Ledger_Identity_Relationship_Architecture_v2.md`

This document is authoritative for:

- identity
- athlete/coach relationships
- onboarding
- billing and entitlement
- authentication and routing
- role/workspace state
- web/mobile account-state behavior
- regression prevention
- production promotion

Strength Ledger is a production platform. No implementation is complete unless
it preserves the platform laws in that document and validates the blast radius
for the affected state machine.

For any task touching auth, onboarding, billing, routing, role access,
workspace mode, athlete-coach linking, mobile/web navigation, or protected
product access, explicitly answer before closing the task:

- What state machine did this touch?
- What adjacent systems could be affected?
- What existing workflows were validated?
- Why can we be confident this did not introduce a regression?

Compile/type checks are required, but they are not sufficient validation.

## Visual Convergence Handoff Gate

For storyboard, reference-convergence, and other material UI work, visual
quality is an acceptance requirement. Before handoff, Codex must:

1. implement the requested experience in canonical DEV;
2. inspect actual mobile screenshots from the canonical `npm start` runtime;
3. compare the implementation directly with the supplied reference;
4. identify the three weakest visual areas;
5. correct those weaknesses; and
6. capture and inspect a second screenshot pass before declaring completion.

For web palette changes and new web components, run `npm run test:web-palette`.
All web chrome, inline styles and generated UI must resolve through the canonical
`app/static/css/00_tokens.css` palette; keep status and chart meanings distinct.
Do not introduce page-owned hardcoded palettes. Retain a deliberate old-color
injection showing the audit rejects the failure class. This does not authorize
changes to artwork, mail, account access or any feature's controls/behavior.

For Athlete Performance Analysis UI changes, run `npm run test:analysis-controls` against the actual served DEV/candidate with the intended authenticated fixture. Retain its rendered control measurements and reproduce undersized controls using `ANALYSIS_REPRODUCE_SMALL_CONTROLS=1`; the guard must reject them. This check is part of the affected web DEV/release validation; do not substitute source-only size assertions or shrink targets for compact columns.

Functional correctness alone is not completion for visual work. Do not hand
over a screen that is generic, prototype-looking, poorly balanced, excessively
empty, cramped, visually inconsistent, badly cropped, difficult to read, or
materially weaker than the supplied specification.

Do not create containers merely because multiple data points exist. Compose
information according to hierarchy, density, and mobile readability. If the
supplied reference is materially more polished than the implementation,
convergence is not complete.

## Canonical Movement Identity Completion Law

A current Session item has one authoritative movement ID:
`WorkoutItem.movement_definition_id`. Real Swap X→Y persists Y. Same Swap
X→X is a semantic no-op. Initial hydration and mutation responses use the same
normalization, and titles, taxonomy, artwork, anatomy, equipment policy and
history all start from that ID. Current effective/performed compatibility
aliases are derived outputs, never competing identity owners.

Original prescription and authorized transitions are append-only provenance.
SetLog performed snapshots remain immutable historical evidence. Neither may
be repurposed as a live movement selector. Reads must not repair Session
identity or persist side effects. See `docs/SINGLE_CURRENT_MOVEMENT_ID.md` for
writer boundaries, migration, invariants and the TestFlight compatibility limit.

Production transfer of a consolidated movement catalog must combine every
governed source history under its surviving ID, without rewriting or double
counting performed sets. Follow `docs/MOVEMENT_CATALOG_PRODUCTION_TRANSFER.md`
and archive passing destination-database reconciliation and read-only receipts
before release closeout; DEV numeric IDs and data must never be copied over.

## Canonical Dynamic Anatomy System Law

Full-figure muscle anatomy is one registered aggregate-evidence system. Every
Session, week, muscle-group, athlete-summary, post-Session, and coach aggregate
must render through `powerlift_mobile/components/anatomy/MuscleMap.tsx`, normally
via `ProgrammingMuscleRegionArt` or `GovernedMuscleThumbnail`. Screens may size
their container, but may not own overlay geometry, load anatomy masters, or
apply corrective figure transforms.

Each masculine/feminine and front/rear master owns independently registered
paths in the same 418 × 941 coordinate space as that master. Highlights must be
silhouette-clipped, must preserve the master's texture and seams, and must keep
primary/secondary semantics distinct. Do not transfer geometry between
presentations with scale or translation, and do not approximate a muscle with
generic circles, ellipses, or rectangles.

One exact movement is not an aggregate anatomy surface. It remains governed by
the Individual Movement Artwork Law and `CanonicalMovementArtwork`; never
replace exact movement identity with a full-body figure.

Any anatomy-system change must update the consumer inventory in
`powerlift_mobile/docs/CANONICAL_DYNAMIC_ANATOMY_SYSTEM.md`, pass the executable
consumer-convergence guard, and complete the DEV visual gate across masculine
front/rear, feminine front/rear, a dense state, and thumbnail/card/hero sizes.

## Core Variants Ledger Law

Core Variants are organized by their governed parent Core lift (Squat, Bench
Press, or Deadlift), never by muscle group. Each exact variant owns an
independent performed-set history. Variant evidence may contextualize
supplemental training, but it must never be merged into or presented as causal
improvement of the parent Competition lift.

The primary Variants surface is continuous and curated: active rotation,
family context, meaningful exact-task progress, block exposure, and the
historical record belong in one chapter. Exhaustive evidence belongs in exact
variant drill-downs. Membership, progress, and source evidence must resolve
from governed Core movement IDs and immutable performed identity, never from
display names, broad families, or inferred carryover.

## Human Movement Artwork Approval Law

Read `docs/DEV_MOVEMENT_ART_REVIEW.md` before generating, replacing, or promoting
movement artwork. Register generated candidates as pending and stop for human
review in the DEV review tool. Codex and automated tools must never approve real
candidates through the browser, HTTP, service calls, or direct manifest edits.
Workflow tests belong only in the isolated, non-promotable QA store.

An explicit owner instruction in the current conversation to mark one exact image
approved may be transcribed as `explicit_owner_chat`, retaining the exact message,
conversation ID and image hash. This records the owner's decision, never an agent
quality judgment; do not impersonate an authenticated UI reviewer. It grants no
approval to other candidates and does not authorize a release.

Regenerate only the latest rejected candidates, using their exact written
rejection reasons. Approved candidates remain locked until the human explicitly
reopens them. Preserve candidate versions and decision history. Promotion must
pass the exact human-approval receipt and content-hash guards. The one-time
preserved-existing-DEV backfill exception authorizes only its original bytes;
it never authorizes a new image or substitutes for human approval.

## Accessory PR backend release requirement

Read `docs/ACCESSORY_PR_BACKEND_RELEASE.md` before accessory PR or shared-backend
release work. Machine PR history MUST remain within the exact equipment/manufacturer context;
Other is separate and first machine baselines stay quiet. The latest isolation
directive supersedes the earlier cross-brand scope. Ship required server changes by validated
Git push from the Production repo to SCUI/main. Client OTA/local tests do not
prove server delivery. The source-bound shared-backend push guard and frozen
2.0.2 compatibility checks are mandatory; verify the live serving SHA afterward.
